# Copy to .dev.vars for local development. .dev.vars is gitignored.
#
# This file is read twice. Locally it is the template for .dev.vars. And when a repository
# is deployed through Cloudflare's "Deploy to Cloudflare" flow, the uncommented names here
# are what the flow asks for before it deploys, and the values it stores on the new Worker.
# So an uncommented line means "every instance needs to decide this", and everything
# optional stays commented out with the instructions beside it. Nothing here may carry a
# value that would be wrong on a real instance.

# Required. Signs every session cookie; a Worker without it cannot sign anyone in, and
# wrangler.toml declares it required so a deploy refuses to go out without one.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# OAuth providers, by convention rather than by a list.
#
# Any of the thirty-five providers Better Auth ships is enabled by setting both halves of
# its credential pair — <PROVIDER>_CLIENT_ID and <PROVIDER>_CLIENT_SECRET — and disabled by
# removing either. Nothing in the repository names them, so adding Discord or Microsoft or
# Slack needs no code change and no fork; the sign-in screen draws whatever /api/config
# reports.
#
#   apple atlassian cognito discord dropbox facebook figma github gitlab google
#   huggingface kakao kick line linear linkedin microsoft naver notion paybin paypal
#   polar railway reddit roblox salesforce slack spotify tiktok twitch twitter vercel
#   vk wechat zoom
#
# Providers wanting more than a pair are configured through the same two names: Apple's
# "secret" is a JWT you generate from a team id, key id and signing key, and goes in
# APPLE_CLIENT_SECRET.
#
# Callback URLs use the SPA origin, not the Worker port: APP_URL is the SPA and the Vite
# proxy forwards /api to the Worker. In local dev Better Auth derives redirect_uri from the
# request Host, so Tailscale MagicDNS / 100.x work too — register each origin you use. The
# path is always /api/auth/callback/<provider>.
#
# The SPA port is 5173 in the main checkout. A git worktree runs on a port derived from its
# directory name, which `pnpm dev` prints — a provider matches the redirect URI exactly, so
# social sign-in from a worktree means registering that port too. Password sign-in and the
# code printed to the Worker console work on any port without registering anything.
#
# Google:  https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   Authorized redirect URI: http://localhost:5173/api/auth/callback/google
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# GitHub:  https://github.com/settings/developers
#   Authorization callback URL: http://localhost:5173/api/auth/callback/github
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# The Android app signs in through Credential Manager, which returns an ID token issued for
# whichever client id it asked for. Set this only if a build asks for its own Android
# audience rather than reusing the web client id above; auth.ts then accepts both.
# GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=

# Passkeys are offered unless this is '0'. They need nothing registered anywhere — the other
# half of WebAuthn is the browser and the user's own device — so this exists for an operator
# who would rather not offer them, not because they can be misconfigured.
# AUTH_PASSKEY=0

# Password sign-up for the test suite and `pnpm seed:demo` is not a line here. `pnpm dev`
# passes ALLOW_TEST_CREDENTIALS=1 to the Worker itself, so it is on for every local server
# and cannot be copied into a deployed one — this file used to carry it, and a file that a
# deploy flow reads must not.

# Email-and-password sign-in, which usually needs no setting at all.
#
# It is offered by default when nothing else on the instance can create an account — no
# OAuth credentials and no mail — because the two methods left in that case cannot start
# one: a passkey is added from an account you already have, and a code needs somewhere to
# send it. So a self-hoster who configures nothing still gets in, and the default withdraws
# the moment a provider or a mail key appears. unbent.app has providers and never sees it.
#
# '1' asks for a password alongside a configured provider. '0' refuses the default, for an
# instance that wants passkeys and nothing else for people who already have accounts — it
# is the one setting here that can leave nobody a way in, so it has to be asked for.
#
# ALLOWED_EMAILS gates password sign-ups the same as OAuth ones.
# AUTH_PASSWORD=1

# Comma-separated emails allowed to create an account. Empty or unset means anyone can
# sign up, which is what local development and the test suite want — the suite signs up
# throwaway actors and would fail against an allowlist. Deployed instances set this so a
# public URL does not become a free notes host for anyone who finds it.
#
# Left empty on a deployed instance, the Worker warns once in its log and names the methods
# a stranger could use. It is a warning rather than a refusal because requiring an allowlist
# to sign up would lock an operator out of their own fresh deployment.
#
# Uncommented so that a deploy flow asks. Empty is right for local development.
ALLOWED_EMAILS=

# '1' says an empty ALLOWED_EMAILS above is deliberate, which silences that warning and does
# nothing else — an instance is already open to anyone the moment the allowlist is empty.
#
# It exists because an absent allowlist cannot say which of two things it means. Without
# this, an instance that chose to be public logs the same warning forever as one that forgot
# to set the list, and a warning nobody can act on is one nobody reads — including on the
# instance that really did forget. No effect locally, where the warning never fires anyway.
# PUBLIC_SIGNUPS=1

# Resend, for password reset mail. Both must be set before any mail is sent; with either
# missing the reset link is printed to the Worker console instead, which is what local
# development wants — the whole flow can be exercised without signing up for anything.
# A deployed instance must set these, or nobody who forgets a password can get back in.
#
#   https://resend.com/api-keys
#   EMAIL_FROM's domain has to be verified with Resend first.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=Unbent <noreply@mail.example.com>
#
# Optional. Mail is usually sent from a subdomain kept separate from the apex, so that a
# problem with it cannot harm the reputation of the address people actually write to — but
# that subdomain is not a mailbox, so Reply on a noreply@ From goes nowhere. Set this to
# the address that does read mail. Unset means Reply goes to EMAIL_FROM.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=hello@example.com

# Who runs this instance.
#
# The built-in privacy policy, terms and "how to delete your account" page name a legal
# entity and give an address to write to. Both of these must be set before those documents
# render at all: unset, /privacy and /terms say this instance has not published a policy,
# and nothing links to them from the sign-in screen or the account menu.
#
# That is deliberate rather than unfinished. These documents used to name one company from
# a constant in the source, which meant every copy of this repository published somebody
# else's legal entity on its own domain — a claim that operator never made. Half a pair is
# ignored the same way an OAuth credential pair is: a policy naming a company with no way to
# reach it is worse than one that admits there is none here.
#
# The software's name stays "Unbent" throughout either way. That is what this program is
# called, not who is answerable for running it.
# OPERATOR_NAME=Example Co
# OPERATOR_EMAIL=help@example.com

# Legal documents you host yourself, if you would rather not use the built-in ones.
#
# Both or neither, and when both are set they win over OPERATOR_NAME/OPERATOR_EMAIL above:
# an operator who has published their own documents is the one to believe. The built-in
# pages then say so and link onward rather than answering 404, because a store listing may
# point at one of them.
#
# Worth knowing before you use the built-in documents: they say the service is hosted in the
# United States and that Minnesota law governs the terms. Neither is configurable. If either
# is wrong for you, these two are the way out.
# LEGAL_PRIVACY_URL=https://example.com/privacy
# LEGAL_TERMS_URL=https://example.com/terms

# The Android app-links document, served at /.well-known/assetlinks.json: which apps, by
# package name and signing certificate, this origin vouches for. Android reads it to open
# links in the app and to offer passkeys to it. Set it only if you build the Android app
# for this instance; unset, the origin vouches for none. The value is the document itself,
# a JSON array of statements — see the README's Android section for the fingerprints.
# ANDROID_ASSET_LINKS=[{"relation":["delegate_permission/common.handle_all_urls"],"target":{"namespace":"android_app","package_name":"com.example.notes","sha256_cert_fingerprints":["AA:BB:..."]}}]

# Auto-fill: a TypeSafe API key for Jev, which turns on the control on a note that fills
# in its title, notebook, tags and fields from what it and its scans say. Unset — the
# default — means the control is not drawn and nothing is sent anywhere.
#
# Each press sends the note's own text (title, body, and the text extracted from its
# PDFs) plus the names of the notebooks, tags and fields, with a few titles from each
# notebook and tag as examples, to api.typesafe.ai. See src/lib/autofill.ts for exactly
# what. A key of its own for development keeps its usage apart from production's.
#
#   https://console.typesafe.ai/keys
# TYPESAFE_API_KEY=
#
# Where those requests go instead, for a stand-in server during development. Leave unset.
# TYPESAFE_API_URL=

# A language model: an Anthropic API key, which turns on reading pictures — and scans with
# no text layer — with a vision model, and writing Auto-fill's titles. What a file is and
# shows, then any text on it, becomes the file's text for search and Auto-fill; a title is
# written from the note whole rather than picked off a line of it. Unset — the default —
# means a picture has no text, as before, titles come from Jev's pick of a line, and
# nothing is sent anywhere.
#
# Each file is read once, after its upload or from the Import panel's "Index existing
# files", and the bytes go to api.anthropic.com; a title is one small request per press of
# Auto-fill. See src/lib/llm.ts for exactly what is asked. A key of its own for development
# keeps its usage apart from production's.
#
#   https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# The model asked. Unset means the default in src/lib/llm.ts, the most capable current
# one; a cheaper one, such as claude-sonnet-5, trades some reading accuracy for cost.
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=
#
# Where those requests go instead, for a stand-in server during development. Leave unset.
# ANTHROPIC_API_URL=

# PostHog, for grouping the browser errors that /api/client-errors otherwise only logs.
#
# Optional, and off here on purpose: a self-hosted instance downloads no SDK, sends nothing
# to anyone, and its privacy policy does not mention an error-tracking service. Setting the
# key is what turns all three on. Errors only — page views are counted by the hosting
# provider, and this captures no page views, no session recordings and no profiles.
#
# The token is public and write-only; it ships in the page either way.
#
#   https://us.posthog.com/settings/project#variables
# POSTHOG_KEY=phc_...
#
# Where the Worker forwards it. Defaults to PostHog's US cloud; set it for the EU cloud
# (https://eu.i.posthog.com) or your own PostHog. The browser never sees this — it talks to
# /ingest on this origin, which is what keeps content blockers from dropping the reports.
# POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
